\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{The factor of i in the doubling elements}
\date{2026-11-15}

\p{Sharatchandra, Thun and Weisz write
#{M_\mu=\ui\gamma_5\gamma_\mu}
\citet{(2.17), p. 209}{sharatchandrathunweisz1981susskind},
with #{\gamma_5} self-adjoint of square #{1}
\citet{(2.18), p. 209}{sharatchandrathunweisz1981susskind}.
Their #{\gamma_\mu} correspond to the generators
#{\varepsilon_\mu^{S}\otimes\bbI_F} of \ref{fdip-009R}, and the
ordered product of those generators is the pseudoscalar
#{\psI} of \ref{fdip-00R3}. In dimension #{4}, their formula
#{\gamma_5=\gamma_1\gamma_2\gamma_3\gamma_4}
\citet{(A.10), p. 235}{sharatchandrathunweisz1981susskind}
corresponds to #{\psI}, of square #{+1}. Then
#{\psI(\varepsilon_\mu^{S}\otimes\bbI_F)} has square #{-1},
and the factor #{\ui} in their #{M_\mu} only makes that
element self-adjoint of square #{+1}. In dimension #{2},
their formula #{\gamma_5=-\ui\gamma_1\gamma_2}
\citet{(A.3), p. 234}{sharatchandrathunweisz1981susskind}
is not #{\psI}. Their
#{M_\mu=\ui\gamma_5\gamma_\mu} then equals
#{\gamma_1\gamma_2\gamma_\mu}, which corresponds to
#{\psI(\varepsilon_\mu^{S}\otimes\bbI_F)}, already
self-adjoint of square #{+1}, and no factor #{\ui} remains.
In both dimensions, conjugation by
#{\psI(\varepsilon_\mu^{S}\otimes\bbI_F)} sends
#{\varepsilon_\nu^{S}\otimes\bbI_F} to its negative when
#{\nu=\mu} and fixes it otherwise. A central factor #{\ui}
does not change the conjugation. Their product #{M_g} of the
elements #{M_\mu} therefore implements the same signs. Their
#{\pi_g} is the corner shift, with component #{\pi/a} on each
axis in the subset and component #{0} otherwise, where #{a} is
their lattice spacing, and their #{a_\mu} is the axis step of
that spacing
\citet{(2.10), p. 208}{sharatchandrathunweisz1981susskind}.
Their printed formula
#{M_g^{\dagger}\gamma_\mu M_g=\ue^{\ui a_\mu\pi_g}\gamma_\mu}
multiplies #{\gamma_\mu} by the phase of that pairing, which
is #{(-1)} when the axis lies in the subset and #{(+1)}
otherwise
\citet{(2.19), p. 209}{sharatchandrathunweisz1981susskind}.
A corner
#{\epsilon} is their subset #{g=\{\mu:\epsilon_\mu=1\}}, and
conjugation by the product of the elements
#{\psI(\varepsilon_\mu^{S}\otimes\bbI_F)} over #{\mu\in g}
gives the signs #{(-1)^{\epsilon_\nu}} of \ref{fdip-009R}.
The identification of their #{\gamma_5} with #{\psI} is stated
for dimension #{4} only.}
